==Controversies==
===Betting===
As the show was pre-recorded, the winner of the show would have been known by those involved in the show. Bookmaker [[Ladbrokes]] halted betting on the show after "a run of bets" was placed for one contestant, many at shops in the [[Ipswich]] area.<ref>[https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-suffolk-33801090 Great British Bake Off bets halted by Ladbrokes – BBC News<!-- Bot generated title -->]</ref>

It was also claimed that dozens of employees of BBC as well as the production company, Love Productions, had opened gambling accounts to place bets on the eventual winner.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/bbc/11913626/Great-British-Bake-Off-rocked-by-10000-betting-scandal-allegations.html |title=Great British Bake Off rocked by £10,000 betting scandal allegations|author= Camilla Turner|date=6 Oct 2015 |work=Daily Telegraph }}</ref>

===Spoilers===
In a [[BBC Radio 2]] broadcast, judge Mary Berry accidentally revealed the eliminated contestant prior to the airing of episode three.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2015/aug/19/mary-berry-blurts-name-next-great-british-bake-off-contestant-get-chop|title=Dough! Mary Berry blurts out name of next Bake Off contestant to get chop|last=Gayle|first=Damien|date=19 August 2015|work=[[The Guardian]]|accessdate=7 September 2015}}</ref>

===Contestants===
Contestant Marie Campbell, who was the first Star Baker of the series, was criticised for being "semi-professional", after it emerged that she had been trained in Ecole Escoffier at the [[Hôtel Ritz Paris|Ritz]] in Paris where she specialised in patisserie. A statement from the BBC said: "There are strict criteria to take part in the show and Marie met those criteria. She gained a certificate for one week’s training in Paris in 1984, more than 10 years ago. She has not worked professionally as a baker or chef."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/11792712/Great-British-Bake-Off-favourite-has-professional-training.html |title=Great British Bake Off favourite 'has professional training' |author=Elizabeth Roberts |date=9 Aug 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.ok.co.uk/tv/great-british-bake-off-2015-controversy-star-baker-marie-campbell |title=Great British Bake Off hit by controversy as Marie Campbell has had 'professional training' |author=Deepika Rajani |date=9 Aug 2015 |work=OK |archive-date=13 August 2015 |access-date=9 August 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150813000346/http://www.ok.co.uk/tv/great-british-bake-off-2015-controversy-star-baker-marie-campbell |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Post-show career==

Nadiya Hussain regularly appears on ''[[The One Show]]'' on BBC1. Her own cookery show, [[The Chronicles of Nadiya]] started on 24 August 2016 on [[BBC One]]. Hussain writes a weekly column for ''The Times Magazine'', a part of the Saturday supplement of ''[[The Times]]'',<ref name=Times-Next-2015>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/life-style/property-home/article/what-nadiya-did-next-mpvpnhv337d|author=Louise Carpenter|title=What Nadiya did next|date=15 November 2015|newspaper=[[The Times]]|access-date=15 November 2015}}</ref><ref name=Metro-Column-2015>{{cite web|last1=Waring|first1=Olivia|url=http://metro.co.uk/2015/11/13/great-british-bake-off-champ-nadiya-jamir-hussain-lands-her-berry-own-column-5499328/|title=Great British Bake Off champ Nadiya Jamir Hussain lands her Berry own column|date=13 November 2015|work=[[Metro (British newspaper)|Metro]]|accessdate=13 November 2015}}</ref><ref name=HuffPo-GBBO-2015>{{cite web|last1=Welsh|first1=Daniel|url=http://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/2015/11/13/great-british-bake-off-winner-nadiya-the-times-magazine-column_n_8553460.html|title='Great British Bake Off' Winner Nadiya Hussain Lands Weekly Cookery Role in the Times Magazine|date=13 November 2015|work=[[Huffington Post]]|accessdate=13 November 2015}}</ref> as well as a monthly column in ''[[Essentials (magazine)|Essentials]]'' magazine.<ref name=essentialsnadiya-2015>{{cite web|url=http://www.goodtoknow.co.uk/recipes/545010/nadiyabakes |title=#NadiyaBakes |date=28 December 2015|work=Essentials|accessdate=28 December 2015}}</ref> Her recipes have also appeared in BBC's ''[[Good Food]]'' magazine.<ref name=goodfood-2015>{{cite web|author=Hussain, Nadiya|url=http://www.bbcgoodfood.com/recipes/christmas-white-chocolate-traybake |title=Christmas white chocolate traybake |date=December 2015|work=[[Good Food]]|accessdate=20 December 2015}}</ref>

Hussain was signed by UK publisher [[Michael Joseph (publisher)|Michael Joseph]], part of Penguin Random House,<ref name=Bookseller-Nadiya-PublishingDeal>{{cite web|last1=Cowdrey|first1=Katherine|url=http://www.thebookseller.com/news/2015-gbbo-winner-nadiya-publish-michael-joseph-318734|title=GBBO winner Nadiya cuts deal with Michael Joseph|date=17 December 2015|work=[[The Bookseller]]|accessdate=20 December 2015}}</ref> for her debut book ''Nadiya's Kitchen'', which is a collection of the recipes that she cooks for friends and family.<ref name=RadioTimes-Nadiya-book>{{cite web|last1=Godwin|first1=Richard|url=http://www.radiotimes.com/news/2016-06-19/nadiya-hussain-continues-cooking-up-success-post-bake-off|title=Nadiya Hussain continues cooking up success post-Bake Off|date=19 June 2016 |work=[[Radio Times]]|accessdate=19 June 2016}}</ref> Hussain has written a children's book of stories and recipes, ''Bake Me A Story'', due to be published by [[Hodder Children's Books]],<ref name=Bookseller-Nadiya-HodderDeal>{{cite web|last1=Eyre|first1=Charlotte|url=http://www.thebookseller.com/news/gbbos-nadiya-hussain-write-childrens-book-320320|title=GBBO's Nadiya Hussain to write children's book|date=14 January 2016|work=[[The Bookseller]]|accessdate=16 January 2016}}</ref> which blends updated versions of fairytales (poor "Sleepless Beauty" just needs a nice cup of cardamom-infused hot chocolate to break her curse; resourceful Jack wins the giant over with yummy bean patties) with colourful illustrations and child-friendly recipes.<ref name=Telegraph-11Inspiring-2016>{{cite news|last1=Hawkes|first1=Rebecca|title=11 inspiring things that Nadiya Hussain has done since winning the Great British Bake Off|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/tv/2016/08/24/11-inspiring-things-that-nadiya-hussain-has-done-since-winning-t/|accessdate=25 August 2016|work=[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]]|date=24 August 2016}}</ref> She is also writing three contemporary women's fiction novels for [[Harlequin Enterprises|Harlequin]].<ref name=Bookseller-Nadiya-Harlequin>{{cite web|last1=Cowdrey|first1=Katherine|url=http://www.thebookseller.com/news/gbbos-nadiya-hussain-write-womens-fiction-hq-326154|title=GBBO's Nadiya Hussain to write women's fiction for HQ|date=11 April 2016|work=[[The Bookseller]]|accessdate=11 April 2016}}</ref>

Tamal Ray writes a monthly column for ''[[The Guardian]]''.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2016/feb/17/tamal-ray-bake-off-dating-friends-recipes |title=Tamal Ray: 'Everyone expects three-tier eclair towers and immaculate millefeuille' |author=Tamal Ray |work=The Guardian |date=7 February 2016 }}</ref>  He presented a television show on health on Channel 4 titled ''Be Your Own Doctor''.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/tv/2016/10/24/tamal-ray-on-life-after-the-great-british-bake-off-and-his-new-t/ |title=Tamal Ray on life after The Great British Bake Off and his new TV show Be Your Own Doctor |first= Catherine |last=Gee |date=24 October 2016 |work=The Telegraph }}</ref> He also participated in the 2018 rendition of ''The Great New Year's Bake Off''.

Flora Shedden studied for a year at the [[University of Edinburgh]] but deferred on a course on art history at the [[University of St Andrews]] for a year to concentrate on writing a cookbook, ''Gatherings'', published in January 2017.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.thecourier.co.uk/fp/lifestyle/entertainment/167599/great-british-bake-off-star-flora-recipe-success/ |title= The Great British Bake Off star Flora has the recipe for success |first= Jack|last= McKeown |date= 6 May 2016|work=The Courier}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.thebookseller.com/news/gbbo-contestant-flora-sheddens-debut-mitchell-beazley-402291 |title=GBBO contestant Flora Shedden's debut snapped up|date=29 September 2016 |first= Katherine |last=Cowdrey|work=The Bookseller }}</ref> She wrote a column on baking for ''[[The Scotsman]]''.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://foodanddrink.scotsman.com/author/flora-shedden/ |title=Flora Snedden |work=The Scotsman }}</ref>

In 2017, Paul Jagger and Sandy Docherty participated in the year's renditions of ''The Great Christmas Bake Off'' and ''The Great New Year's Bake Off'' respectively, with the former also winning the special's star baker.

==Ratings==
According to the overnight ratings, the opening episode of the series 6 of ''Bake Off'' was watched by 9.3 million viewers, a rise of over 2 million from the previous year.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-33800711 |title=Great British Bake Off opener watched by 9.3m viewers|date=6 August 2015 |work=BBC }}</ref> The final was watched by an overnight audience of 13.4 million viewers peaking at 14.5, the highest overnight total of 2015.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2015/oct/08/the-great-british-bake-off-final-nadiya-jamir-hussain-gbbo|title=The Great British Bake Off final gets biggest TV audience of the year|work=The Guardian |author=Mark Sweney |date= 8 October 2015 }}</ref> The list of ten most-watched television programmes of 2015 was also dominated by ''The Great British Bake Off'', with seven of the year's ten being episodes of The Great British Bake Off.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/media/12054696/Great-British-Bake-Off-dominates-list-of-most-watched-shows-of-2015.html |title=Great British Bake Off dominates list of most-watched shows of 2015 |author= Patrick Foster|date= 17 December 2015 |work=The Daily Telegraph }}</ref> During the entire length of the series, no national programme on any of the episodes was higher rated than Bake Off.

Official episode viewing figures are from [[BARB]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.barb.co.uk/whats-new/weekly-top-30?_s=4|title=Weekly Top 30 Programmes|publisher=[[Broadcasters' Audience Research Board]]|accessdate=16 September 2015}}</ref>

